CONTRACT DEFINITION:
1. RESEARCH NOTE: See plan/002_32eb66cd705d/bugfix/001_f635210a7f29/architecture/issue_analysis.md §Issue 1. The current handleError in FormStackRenderer (src/components/FormStackRenderer.tsx:67-69) does entry.deferred.reject(error) then onClose(). This violates PRD §9. The render-error path (FormErrorBoundary) is already compliant — it shows Retry/Dismiss without mutating the stack. The fix makes the form-invoked onError path consistent by routing to the boundary's showError() method (from P1.M1.T1.S1).
2. INPUT: FormErrorBoundary now has a public showError(error: Error) method (from P1.M1.T1.S1). The FormStackRenderer maps over InternalStackEntry<unknown>[] and wraps each in a FormErrorBoundary with onDismiss={() => { entry.deferred.resolve(undefined); onClose(); }}.
3. LOGIC:
   a) Add a ref Map to FormStackRenderer: `const boundaryRefs = useRef(new Map<string, FormErrorBoundary>());`
   b) In the JSX map, add a ref callback to each FormErrorBoundary: `ref={(instance) => { if (instance) boundaryRefs.current.set(entry.id, instance); else boundaryRefs.current.delete(entry.id); }}`
   c) Replace handleError: instead of `entry.deferred.reject(error); onClose();`, do:
      ```ts
      const handleError = (error: unknown) => {
        const err = error instanceof Error ? error : new Error(String(error));
        console.error(`[FormStack] Form-invoked onError in form ${entry.id}:`, err);
        boundaryRefs.current.get(entry.id)?.showError(err);
        // NO reject, NO onClose — form stays mounted, stack unchanged,
        // openForm() stays pending (PRD §9 compliant).
      };
      ```
   d) The existing onDismiss handler on FormErrorBoundary is correct: when the user clicks Dismiss after seeing the error, it resolves the deferred with undefined (cancel semantics) and pops the form. When the user clicks Retry, the boundary clears hasError and the form remounts — the deferred is still pending.
   e) Import FormErrorBoundary type for the ref Map type annotation.
4. OUTPUT: FormStackRenderer.handleError now routes form-invoked errors to the boundary's error UI without mutating the stack or rejecting the promise. The form-invoked onError path is now consistent with the render-error path (both show Retry/Dismiss, neither auto-pops).
5. MOCKING: In the integration test, mock the form component to call its onError prop on a button click. Track the openForm() promise for rejection (it should NOT reject). Track unhandledRejection events (should NOT fire). Assert stack-length stays 1 after onError fires. Assert the error boundary fallback UI is visible (getByRole('alert')).
6. DOCS: [Mode A] Update the FormProps.onError JSDoc in src/types/form.ts to clarify that onError surfaces the error to the FormErrorBoundary (inline Retry/Dismiss UI) and does NOT reject openForm() or mutate the stack — aligning with PRD §9.

CONTRACT DEFINITION:
1. RESEARCH NOTE: See plan/002_32eb66cd705d/bugfix/001_f635210a7f29/architecture/issue_analysis.md §Issue 2 and architecture/system_context.md §Confirmation System. The FormStackProvider (src/components/FormStackProvider.tsx) uses a single useState<PendingConfirmation | null> slot. PendingConfirmation currently has { affectedForms: string[]; resolve: (confirmed: boolean) => void }. Both cancelForm() (via handleCancelRequest, :213-217) and popToIndex() (:188-191) call requestConfirmation. The handlers handleConfirmationConfirm and handleConfirmationCancel (:230-243) read pendingConfirmation from closure and call .resolve(true/false).
2. INPUT: No inputs from other subtasks. This is a self-contained fix within FormStackProvider.tsx. The ConfirmationDialog component's title logic reads pendingConfirmation.affectedForms.length for pluralization — this still works with merged affected forms.
3. LOGIC:
   a) Change the PendingConfirmation interface: replace `resolve: (confirmed: boolean) => void` with `resolvers: Set<(confirmed: boolean) => void>`.
   b) Change requestConfirmation to use the functional setState form so it can coalesce:
      ```ts
      const requestConfirmation = useCallback((affectedForms: string[]): Promise<boolean> => {
        return new Promise((resolve) => {
          setPendingConfirmation((prev) => {
            if (prev) {
              return {
                affectedForms: [...new Set([...prev.affectedForms, ...affectedForms])],
                resolvers: new Set([...prev.resolvers, resolve]),
              };
            }
            return { affectedForms, resolvers: new Set([resolve]) };
          });
        });
      }, []);
      ```
   c) Change handleConfirmationConfirm and handleConfirmationCancel to iterate the Set and resolve all waiters with the same answer, using functional setState (this also makes them stable — remove pendingConfirmation from their useCallback dependency arrays):
      ```ts
      const handleConfirmationConfirm = useCallback(() => {
        setPendingConfirmation((prev) => { prev?.resolvers.forEach((r) => r(true)); return null; });
      }, []);
      const handleConfirmationCancel = useCallback(() => {
        setPendingConfirmation((prev) => { prev?.resolvers.forEach((r) => r(false)); return null; });
      }, []);
      ```
   d) The ConfirmationDialog's title prop logic in the JSX references `pendingConfirmation.affectedForms.length` — this still works because the field name is unchanged. Verify the plural title ('Discard Changes to N Forms?') works when forms are merged.
4. OUTPUT: Concurrent cancelForm()/popToIndex() calls that trigger confirmation now coalesce onto a single dialog. All tracked promises settle when the user responds. No promise is orphaned.
5. MOCKING: Write an integration test: render FormStackProvider with a confirmOnCancel:true form. Call cancelForm() twice rapidly (two promises in flight). Track both via .finally(() => settled[i] = true). Click 'Keep Editing' once. Assert via waitFor that both settled[0] and settled[1] are true within a timeout. Also test the 'Discard' path: both resolve true, stack clears.
6. DOCS: none — no user-facing/config/API surface change. The public behavior (cancel resolves, keep editing doesn't) is unchanged; only the internal re-entrancy bug is fixed.

CONTRACT DEFINITION:
1. RESEARCH NOTE: See plan/002_32eb66cd705d/bugfix/001_f635210a7f29/architecture/issue_analysis.md §Issue 3 and architecture/system_context.md §Type Hierarchy. Currently FormStackViewportValue (src/types/context.ts:19) and FormStackRendererProps (src/components/FormStackRenderer.tsx:24) are structurally identical, both carrying InternalStackEntry<unknown>[]. The FormStackViewportContext (src/context/FormStackContext.ts:37) is typed createContext<FormStackViewportValue | null>. The provider's viewportValue useMemo creates { stack: state.stack, onClose: closeForm, onCancelRequest: handleCancelRequest } which is the internal shape.
2. INPUT: No inputs from other subtasks. This is the type-definition foundation.
3. LOGIC:
   a) In src/types/context.ts, add a new @internal interface for the context value (same shape as the current FormStackViewportValue but explicitly internal):
      ```ts
      /** @internal — carries InternalStackEntry for the renderer; never exposed publicly */
      export interface FormStackViewportContextValue {
        stack: InternalStackEntry<unknown>[];
        onClose: () => void;
        onCancelRequest: (entry: InternalStackEntry<unknown>) => Promise<boolean>;
      }
      ```
   b) Change the public FormStackViewportValue to a sanitized shape:
      ```ts
      export interface FormStackViewportValue {
        /** Read-only stack entries (id + label only — no internal component/deferred) */
        stack: readonly StackEntry[];
        /** Callback to close/pop the top form */
        onClose: () => void;
      }
      ```
      Remove onCancelRequest from the public type (it takes InternalStackEntry which consumers no longer have). Export FormStackViewportContextValue from src/types/index.ts.
   c) In src/context/FormStackContext.ts, change FormStackViewportContext type from `FormStackViewportValue | null` to `FormStackViewportContextValue | null`. Update the import.
4. OUTPUT: Two distinct types: FormStackViewportContextValue (internal, carries InternalStackEntry) for the context, and FormStackViewportValue (public, sanitized to StackEntry[]) for the hook. The context now carries the internal type. The public type no longer exposes component, deferred, confirmOnCancel, or onCancelRequest.
5. DOCS: [Mode A] Update the JSDoc on FormStackViewportValue in src/types/context.ts to document the sanitized fields and explicitly state that component/deferred are internal-only.

CONTRACT DEFINITION:
1. RESEARCH NOTE: See plan/002_32eb66cd705d/bugfix/001_f635210a7f29/architecture/issue_analysis.md §Issue 3 and architecture/test_patterns.md §Issue 3. The hook useFormStackViewport (src/hooks/useFormStackViewport.ts) currently returns useContext(FormStackViewportContext) directly. After P1.M2.T1.S1, the context carries FormStackViewportContextValue (internal, with InternalStackEntry) and the hook must return FormStackViewportValue (public, sanitized). The FormStackViewport component (src/components/FormStackViewport.tsx) reads the context and spreads onto <FormStackRenderer {...viewport} /> — this still works because the context now carries FormStackViewportContextValue which is structurally identical to FormStackRendererProps. The existing test useFormStackViewport.test.tsx has a test 'exposes internal entry fields (component/deferred)' that asserts the leak exists — this test must be replaced with one asserting the sanitized surface. The 'FormStackViewportValue is assignable to FormStackRendererProps' test must also change — the public type is intentionally NOT spreadable onto the renderer anymore.
2. INPUT: FormStackViewportContextValue (internal type) and FormStackViewportValue (sanitized public type) from P1.M2.T1.S1. The context type is now FormStackViewportContextValue | null.
3. LOGIC:
   a) In src/hooks/useFormStackViewport.ts, map the internal context value to the public type:
      ```ts
      import { useContext, useMemo } from 'react';
      import { FormStackViewportContext } from '../context';
      import type { FormStackViewportValue } from '../types';

      export function useFormStackViewport(): FormStackViewportValue | null {
        const internal = useContext(FormStackViewportContext);
        return useMemo(() => {
          if (!internal) return null;
          return {
            stack: internal.stack.map(({ id, label }) => ({ id, label })),
            onClose: internal.onClose,
          };
        }, [internal]);
      }
      ```
   b) Verify FormStackViewport.tsx still compiles — it reads useContext(FormStackViewportContext) (now FormStackViewportContextValue | null) and spreads onto <FormStackRenderer>. FormStackRendererProps is structurally identical to FormStackViewportContextValue, so the spread works. No change needed in FormStackViewport.tsx unless the type inference fails — in that case, add an explicit type annotation.
   c) Update src/hooks/__tests__/useFormStackViewport.test.tsx:
      - Replace the 'exposes internal entry fields' test with a test asserting the sanitized surface: stack entries have ONLY { id, label }, no 'component', no 'deferred', no 'confirmOnCancel'. Assert value.onClose is a function. Assert 'onCancelRequest' is undefined on the returned value.
      - Replace the 'FormStackViewportValue is assignable to FormStackRendererProps' test: the public type is intentionally NOT assignable to FormStackRendererProps (that was the leak). Instead, test that FormStackViewportContextValue IS assignable (internal context → renderer props), and that the hook returns the correct sanitized shape.
   d) Run tsc --noEmit and vitest to verify all types and tests pass.
4. OUTPUT: useFormStackViewport() returns a sanitized FormStackViewportValue with stack: readonly StackEntry[] (id + label only). Consumers cannot access component, deferred, confirmOnCancel, or onCancelRequest. The <FormStackViewport/> component still renders correctly via the internal context.
5. DOCS: [Mode A] Update the JSDoc on useFormStackViewport in src/hooks/useFormStackViewport.ts to reflect the sanitized return type. Update the example to show only { stack, onClose } access.

CONTRACT DEFINITION:
1. RESEARCH NOTE: See plan/002_32eb66cd705d/bugfix/001_f635210a7f29/architecture/issue_analysis.md §Issue 4. The openForm function in FormStackProvider (src/components/FormStackProvider.tsx, around line 100) currently has a useCallback dependency array of [] (empty — it does not read state.stack). Adding a dev-mode duplicate-ID check requires reading state.stack, so the dependency array must include state.stack. This changes openForm's reference stability: it will now be a new reference whenever the stack changes. This is acceptable because openForm is called imperatively (onClick handlers), not used as a useEffect dependency. The existing dev-mode guard pattern (checking process.env?.NODE_ENV === 'development') is used elsewhere in this file (closeForm warning, popToIndex RangeError, viewport mount warnings).
2. INPUT: No inputs from other subtasks. The state.stack is InternalStackEntry<unknown>[] available in the FormStackProvider closure.
3. LOGIC: At the beginning of the openForm useCallback body, before dispatch({ type: 'PUSH_FORM', ... }), add:
   ```ts
   if (typeof process !== 'undefined' && process.env?.NODE_ENV === 'development') {
     if (state.stack.some((e) => e.id === options.id)) {
       console.warn(
         `[FormStack] Duplicate form id "${options.id}" detected. ` +
         'Form IDs should be unique on the stack to avoid React key collisions ' +
         'in FormStackRenderer and Breadcrumbs (PRD §5.2: "Unique identifier for this form instance").'
       );
     }
   }
   ```
   Update the useCallback dependency array from [] to [state.stack].
   Write a failing test: use vi.stubEnv('NODE_ENV', 'development') (and vi.stubGlobal if needed), spy on console.warn, open a form with id 'dup', open another with id 'dup', assert console.warn was called with a message containing 'Duplicate form id'. Then open a form with a unique id and assert no additional warning.
4. OUTPUT: openForm emits a console.warn in development mode when a duplicate id is pushed. No runtime behavior change (the form still pushes; uniqueness is a documented consumer responsibility). Production is unaffected.
5. DOCS: [Mode A] Add a note to the OpenFormOptions.id JSDoc in src/types/stack.ts clarifying that duplicate IDs produce a development-mode warning and can cause React key collisions.
